    {
      "id": "be-s9-exponential-smoothing-c4",
      "heading": "Practice: moving average or exponential smoothing?",
      "explanation_md": "The practice question takes the 2017 to 2025 sales (80, 85, 90, 100, 120, 110, 130, 140, 150) and asks for the 2026 forecast by a **3-year moving average** and by **exponential smoothing with α = 0.1**, then compares them with **RMSE**.\n\nWorked with the slides' own conventions (first actual as the first forecast, RMSE over 2020 to 2025 so both methods cover the same six years):\n\n| Method | 2026 forecast | RMSE |\n|---|---|---|\n| 3-year moving average | 140 | about 20.07 |\n| Exponential smoothing, α = 0.1 | about 103.21 | about 38.21 |\n\nThe **moving average** wins here. With α as small as 0.1 the smoothed forecast reacts **too slowly** to sales that keep rising, so its errors are large.",
      "example_ids": [],
      "key_terms": [],
      "exam_line": "Compute both forecasts, compute RMSE for both over the same years, and choose the lower RMSE: here the 3-year moving average.",
      "clarification": null,
      "check_questions": [
